WEBVTT

STYLE
::cue {
  background-color: rgba(23, 20, 16, 0.74);
  color: #f0e9dd;
  font-size: 88%;
  line-height: 1.35;
}

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.942 line:84%
Every factory hides the same problem.

2
00:00:02.972 --> 00:00:08.677 line:84%
In a coffee roastery, a different roast means emptying the drum and starting the profile over.

3
00:00:08.707 --> 00:00:14.122 line:84%
In a brewery, after an IPA you wash the entire line, or you do not brew.

4
00:00:14.152 --> 00:00:18.302 line:84%
In 3D printing, you swap the filament and wait for heat.

5
00:00:18.332 --> 00:00:22.284 line:84%
Different businesses — one question: what order?

6
00:00:22.314 --> 00:00:23.817 line:84%
Here it is with numbers.

7
00:00:23.847 --> 00:00:27.207 line:84%
Five orders, each needing its own tool.

8
00:00:27.237 --> 00:00:34.173 line:84%
Run them as they arrived, and every family change stops the machine — it swaps the tool and produces nothing.

9
00:00:34.203 --> 00:00:35.798 line:84%
Four stops.

10
00:00:35.828 --> 00:00:42.683 line:84%
Now just reorder, grouping the similar ones: same work, two stops, and the shop finishes earlier.

11
00:00:42.713 --> 00:00:45.330 line:84%
But the changeover is only one of the bills.

12
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:52.227 line:84%
There is the lateness on every promised date — and sometimes the cheapest move is refusing the order outright.

13
00:00:52.257 --> 00:00:58.508 line:84%
Count it properly, and the most urgent order, left for last, costs more than everything before it.

14
00:00:58.538 --> 00:01:00.354 line:84%
Order changes time.

15
00:01:00.384 --> 00:01:02.548 line:84%
Time changes cost.

16
00:01:02.578 --> 00:01:05.160 line:84%
So why not test every order?

17
00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:09.549 line:84%
With five jobs there are a hundred and twenty — checkable one by one.

18
00:01:09.579 --> 00:01:12.695 line:84%
And the best one pays the same two changeovers.

19
00:01:12.725 --> 00:01:18.593 line:84%
But at twenty-eight jobs, checking them all would take thirty-five thousand times the age of the universe.

20
00:01:18.623 --> 00:01:20.973 line:84%
This is not about a faster computer.

21
00:01:21.003 --> 00:01:28.310 line:84%
This is where metaheuristics come in: stop looking for the perfect answer, find a very good one fast.

22
00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:38.411 line:84%
Take a sequence, tear a piece out, offer each job back into every free position at its real price, and keep the cheapest — refusing when nothing pays.

23
00:01:38.441 --> 00:01:40.396 line:84%
That loop is one bet.

24
00:01:40.426 --> 00:01:44.483 line:84%
Every method in this field begins by choosing where not to look.

25
00:01:44.513 --> 00:01:47.873 line:84%
Prove it exactly, and the hours run out.

26
00:01:47.903 --> 00:01:52.157 line:84%
Accept only improvements and you stop at the first dead end.

27
00:01:52.187 --> 00:01:56.139 line:84%
Ban going back, and the search walks out of it.

28
00:01:56.169 --> 00:01:59.425 line:84%
Some ruin their own best answer on purpose.

29
00:01:59.455 --> 00:02:04.452 line:84%
Eight ways to spend the time you have, and none is best on every case.

30
00:02:04.482 --> 00:02:09.038 line:84%
The closer the model gets to a real shop, the more branches appear.

31
00:02:09.068 --> 00:02:10.802 line:84%
Which is the good news.

32
00:02:10.832 --> 00:02:16.487 line:84%
It is all below — code, tests, every number, and what did not work.
